WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.080
There are few franchises that have been churning
out movies semi-regularly since the early

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.380
1960s.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:10.110
Even fewer have brought the kind of box office
results that break records.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:14.120
And there's really only one named after a
character so suave that grown men immediately

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.830
feel an overwhelming desire to imitate him
when they put on a tuxedo.

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:21.640
When it comes to these criteria, no one does
it better than...

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.660
“Bond, James Bond."

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.120
Sean Connery was the original "official" James
Bond, bringing the character from the pages

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.150
of intelligence-officer-turned-writer Ian
Fleming's novels to the big screen.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:38.930
His first adventure was 1962's Dr. No, which
cost $1 million to make and earned nearly

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:44.059
$60 million worldwide (a little over $509
million in 2020 money).

00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:49.200
The role brought Connery fame, fortune, and
an eternal association with an iconic character.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.720
But the famously brash and borderline reclusive
Connery - who passed away at the age of 90

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.440
in October, 2020 - wasn't just a member of
the exclusive list of actors who've played

00:00:58.440 --> 00:00:59.440
Bond.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.950
He also has a metaphorical membership to the
club of actors who regret the role that made

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:03.950
them famous.

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:07.610
This is the reason Sean Connery regretted
playing James Bond.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:09.350
Connery was lucky to land the role of Bond.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:13.420
According to the late Richard Maibaum , known
for his screenplay adaptations of the Bond

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:14.420
novels,

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.860
"[Fleming] thought David Niven would have
made a great James Bond."

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.880
Roger Moore was also considered for the part
early on.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:24.170
Connery's bad-boy vibe ultimately won him
the part, but the success of Dr. No quickly

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:27.439
turned his big break into an all-encompassing
headache.

00:01:27.439 --> 00:01:30.479
Connery was dismissive of the Bond films while
he was still making them.

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:35.770
In 1964 - when his third Bond outing, Goldfinger,
was in production - Connery told an interviewer

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:40.630
that Bond was just "a dull, prosaic English
policeman" when all the "exotic touches" were

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:41.630
removed.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:45.170
He could have been demonstrating unique modesty,
but it certainly sounds like he didn't think

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:46.450
very highly of Bond.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:51.450
In November 1965, Connery admitted to a reporter
that he was already "fed up to here with the

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:52.670
whole Bond bit."

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:57.810
Though Connery said at the time that he "shouldn't
knock" Bond - and said in a November 1964

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:01.380
interview with The New York Times that he'd
be "everlastingly grateful" to the character

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.610
- his attitude changed over the years.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:08.290
By the time he was shooting You Only Live
Twice, released in 1967, Connery had fallen

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:12.700
out with producer Albert "Cubby" Broccoli,
who'd been instrumental in getting him hired.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.870
It didn't help that he was stalked and chased
by paparazzi during filming, or that he felt

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.500
his salary was too low.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.319
You Only Live Twice was meant to be Connery's
last Bond appearance, but he returned for

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:26.510
1971's Diamonds Are Forever - though not out
of love for the character.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:31.440
In a 1971 interview with The Guardian, Connery
made it clear that he resented the studio

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.459
executives for wasting his time.

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:36.080
"I'd been [messed] about too much on other
Bond pictures.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.170
There's so much bulls--- that comes from bad
decisions being made at the top."

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:45.220
Connery struck a deal to be paid $10,000 per
week if the film's shoot ran over the scheduled

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:46.220
18 weeks.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:47.220
He added,

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.129
"Talking to some of these moguls about it
is like trying to describe to someone who

00:02:50.129 --> 00:02:53.950
has never taken exercise what it is like to
feel fit when you do exercise.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:54.950
They don't understand."

00:02:54.950 --> 00:03:00.030
Apparently, Connery only agreed to make the
movie (having skipped 1969's On Her Majesty's

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:04.390
Secret Service) because he was given a large
percentage of profits and the opportunity

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.620
to produce two movies of his choice.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:11.010
And Connery wasn't done with Bond money yet:
He returned for the appropriately titled Never

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:14.450
Say Never Again, reportedly for around $5
million.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.610
Amidst all of that, Connery made it clear
how he really felt about the character that

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.110
launched his career, via The Guardian:

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.840
"I have always hated that damned James Bond.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.019
I'd like to kill him."

